  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 243 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure lingers through Sunday with areas \nof frost expected outside the Champlain Valley tonight with \nclear skies and lighter winds pointing to strong radiational \ncooling. By Sunday, winds shift more southwesterly promoting \nwarming with temperatures back around seasonal averages in the \nlow to mid 60s. \n\nA front approaches Sunday night and will move through early \nMonday bringing some light rainfall mainly towards the Canadian \nborder. Less than 0.25\" is expected with low potential for up to\n0.33\" over upslope areas in the northern Greens. Winds increase\nout of the northwest later Monday with gusts likely in the \n20-30 mph range for many locations especially in the northern \nChamplain Valley and along Lake Champlain. These breezes will \nlinger and slowly decrease through Tuesday. Cold air advection \nsupports temperatures back into the upper 40s to upper 50s \nMonday through Tuesday. Freezing lows are probable Monday and \nTuesday nights outside the Champlain and St Lawrence Valleys.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Main impactful weather in the days 4 through 7 \ntimeframe will be potential for freezing temperatures daybreak \nWednesday where a strong surface inversion is present. \nTemperatures remain on track be a garden killer where vegetation\nremains alive, with exceptions potentially in the northern \nChamplain Valley, particularly in the wide valley that is prone \nto see southerly winds mix to the surface. These winds should \ndevelop overnight and moderate temperatures substantially after \ninitially falling off after sunset, as high pressure shifts to \nour southeast overnight. In contrast, southern/eastern portions \nof the Champlain and Connecticut River Valley will be vulnerable\nto colder conditions and frost; the latest probabilities of \nfreezing temperatures are near 15-30%. Meanwhile, throughout the\nAdirondack region and the remainder of central/eastern Vermont,\nthose probabilities are about 40-90%. Where winds remain calm \novernight, temperatures could fall up to a few degrees below \ncurrent forecast values and the lowest of the season so far. \n\nConsensus in the longer range (Wednesday through Friday night) \nshows somewhat unsettled weather with a closed low near Hudson \nBay finally departing towards the weekend. This scenario holds a\nlongwave trough over our region but leaves rain chances higher \nto our north, and likewise in northern portions of northern New \nYork and Vermont. Low level flow will initially be southerly \nleading to a warming trend through at least Thursday, but a \ncouple of cold fronts will probably pass by to support a cooling\ntrend towards next weekend. There is typical increasing spread \nin the temperature range each day, associated with timing and \nmagnitude differences in the air mass change with any front. \nOverall, temperatures will are expected to be fairly typical of \nthis time of year regardless of variability. As we get closer to\nnext weekend the details will become sharper with regards to \nthese fluctuations.",
